A||||11||Shaun41178||Aluminum block has arrived!!!||||10||
Z||000000||Shaun41178||03-31-2000||08:26 PM||||Well it came today.  Thats right I am now the owner of an aluminum block.  All I have to say is that this thing is sweet.  it is a piece of artwork.  4 bolt splayed intermediate bearing caps, billet steel caps, Main studs, already has freeze plugs installed, revised and improved oiling system with three holes for the cam bearings instead of two on the iron block.  It is totally kick ass.  And it weighs 45 pounds less then the iron block which will also allow my car to accelerate faster too!  From my understanding this block isn't even made anymore.  I guess I got lucky huh guys?<P>I plan on using this block with my 3.2 rebuild.  Using head studs too along with the main studs.  I just wish it wasn't such a task to adopt aluminum heads to our Fieros cause then I could say I have an all aluminum engine.  Oh well.  Perhaps if time and money and maybe if lucky, sponsors allow themselves in the future, I will have an all aluminum engine.<P> I have a few pics of it already and if anyone wants to see the block let me know.  It would probably be easier for me to send it to one person and then have them post the pic on this website since I don't know how to use pip.||||reg||10
Z||000001||88formula||03-31-2000||10:09 PM||||Three grand for 45 LB’s, what a shame. Way cool man!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||03-31-2000||11:12 PM||||That block should have a hole to mount a crank sensor.  If a 3.1 crank is installed the internal rib to the hole will touch a connecting rod when it goes by, grinding an eighth inch clearance before installing the other rods will save time I spent putting together and taking apart.<P>I couldn't find studs to mount aluminum heads and am resigned to putting heli-coils and headbolts on.  Did the studs come with the block or is there current source?<P>Enjoy the building and I'll be watching for the startup post.||||reg||12
Z||000003||batboy||04-01-2000||09:28 AM||||Sure wish they made an aftermarket aluminum performance head. Hey Shaun, try to find a pair of those aluminum 86 2.8 heads that Cooter mentioned.||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||04-01-2000||06:05 PM||||So are you going to use a blower,or a handmade tunnelram for induction?<BR>You can't put iron heads on that motor you know,it'd just be wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||04-01-2000||11:42 PM||||Shaun sent me a pic of his block. I gotta tell you it's a work of art. I have tears in my eyes(from jealousy and the glare). Very nice!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Shaun41178||04-02-2000||12:37 PM||||Phaeton thanks for the tip.  I will make to take a look at it.  Head studs are made my ARP but are for the iron heads.  I don't know if they make them for aluminum heads or not.  If you haven't already called them then you should do so as they might make them or ask your local speed shop as they probably have a catalog with all the part numbers and such.<P>yea it will be wrong to put iron heads on but what can I do people?  At this point in time I do not want to swap out my whole wiring harness and find a computer and then have to custom program it with a chip or what not if I decide to go with DIS.  Wiring will be a pain in the @ss and I am not ready for that at this point.  It will probably be hard as hell to find an 86 cavalier intake and heads if it is true that they had clearance for a distributor.  I have never seen one but come on are they exclusive to the model year of 86?  If only made like that for one year the odds of me finding one is slim to none.  No junkyard around here will have it thats for sure.  I guess i could start talking to some J-body people to get the scoop on this whole thing.  But even if I was able to get aluminum heads and that cavalier intake system so I can use my distributor, I still might run into problems as far as fuel rail go, as I will probably need the cavalier one and then the throttle body might not mount right either.  Plus finding a way to mount the head to the dogbone will be another problem as I have been told that the mounting holes are in different locations.  So I might have to fab up all new way of mounting the dogbone and then again with the accesory brackets.  The a/c and alt brackets might not even bolt up which would means I would need different brackets.<P>See what I am saying people?  Way too much research and time to figure all that out.  i want this engine in before June if I can help it.  Thats June of 2000.  I want to be competing for money at drag events this summer and race along those import guys and show them what pontiac could have had. <P>Like I said when time and money allow themselves in the futrure then I will pursue the idea further but right now it is just too far out of reach.||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||04-02-2000||01:23 PM||||WAY TO STEP UP SHAUN! To rich for my blood... ||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||04-02-2000||01:44 PM||||Chris West sells a pair of urethane motor mounts that let you toss the dogbone for $130.Probably last a bit longer than rubber ones too.<BR>For around $900 you can get a fully programable fuel injection system from Simple Digital Systems that has a valet mode,security mode,an onboard controller to make changes on the fly,controls for nitrous,wastegates,ignition advance,and a pretty high rate of information feedback/adjustment.<BR>That way you could run individual runners,and even individual throttle bodies as big as you want.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Eric||04-03-2000||10:26 PM||||Shaun, are you still planning on using all the standard 3.1 components mainly the Federal Mogul Hyper pistons.  Sound's like you have a great oppertunity to build a killer bottom end.  It would really suck if you had to be limited by those pistons.  I've advocated those hypers in the past but now I'm a little concerned.  Even though I never figured out the final compression ratio, I have to guess it is lower than stock 8.9 to 1.  The dish on those pistons is big enough to feed your dog from.  I've tried to rationalize this by saying the increased stroke makes up for it, but I don't really believe that now.  Plus with the nitrous you will be running, I think it would be a good insurance policy to have a more durable forged slug as a substitute.  Things happen fast at 300 horsepower with these little motors.  That being said, I'm not as concerned with the durabilty as much as the lack of compression.  You've held the stock motor together well enough with the juice so you obviously know how to set nitrous up.  If you do use these, have the heads milled as much as possible.  Mine got shaved .010 and the manifolds fit perfectly.  I don't know how far you can go, but that's a good starting point.  Minimal head porting is okay.  You can save a little money here since the nitrous is its own air supply.  Get a cam with good/big exhaust #s for obvious reasons.  What are you doing for a crank, new or reground?  Keep us updated.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Eric||04-03-2000||10:32 PM||||I've been thinking about what I said.  Maybe a lower compression ratio could be a good thing for a nitrous car.  In its own way it's like forced induction extra air plus extra fuel.  Speaking of fuel, what kind of setup are you going to use, Shaun?  Are you sticking with the dry NOS kit?  What injectors 19lbs? ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Shaun41178||04-04-2000||07:01 PM||||I have looked at my pistons and the dish is not too deep in my opinion.  However after thinking it over, the Firebird 3.1 had 8.5-1 compression from the  factory.  Now if these are upgraded pistons for the firebirds then the compression will be around 8.5-1.  Now with a slight milling of the heads and block might bring it up to around 8.7 or so.  I could probably have my machinist run the numbers or I could just do it myself to know for sure.<P>The stock crank is pretty darn strong from the factory already.  They can handle quite a bit of hp.  At least 20 psi has been run on one that I know of and it has held togethor.  I think the stock crank can handle over 300 hp with no problems.  Stock rods are forged from the factory so no problems there as they can handle over 300 hp too.  The hyper pistons should be strong enough to support 300 hp as well.  I have looked at them and they have pretty thick ring lands.  Again any piston can go south if the tuning and air/fuel ratio is not correct even the forged ones.  Plus forged crank and pistons are not in my budget.  $600 for a forged crank or so and $300 for the pistons.  Thats $900 right there, and with the aluminum block and machining to it will make it $1800 right there.  I was only planning on spending in the neighborhood of $1500 and I am over that now with this block.  I want to rebuild a 3.33 tranny as I have one but that will cost $400 and a new torque converter will be $400 for a higher stall speed.  So thats $800 right there.  I doubt that tranny won't get in this summer but if it does it will be like august or september.  Some new info has come up with a Sunbird 125C which apparently has a 3.80 Final drive.  RPM's will be god awefull at highway sppeds but the acceleration will rock like none other.  That tranny with a shift kit and higher stall will give me .3-.5 right there easy in the qtr.  I am going to keep my eyes open for one of them trannies.<P>As far as a crank goes I have one in agreement with a  guy.  It is used and the guy doesn't know how many miles is on it but he says it looks good.  Also he says that if it is a bad crank I can get my money back.  Price for this crank is under $100 so it is a no lose situation if that crank is bad.  If I can't use that crank then I will probably bite the bullet and buy a new one from GM.  Yea $400 bones but I would rather have a new one then a used one that has been machined by some incompetant machinist or one that is in a hurry to pump them out.<P>I wouldn't mind a compression ratio of under 8.9-1 as that would go good with a turbo or nitrous application.  I am going to use the stock Fiero fuel setup and rail.  I am not going to get an adjustable regulator as the stock one will work just fine.  I might get one later however just for fine tuning to adjust for max power on a dyno.  I am going to go with 19# injectors and am sticking with the dry nos kit.  I will start with a 70 shot and if I feel confident I will up to 100 shot during competitions.  Those hyper pistons can take a 100 shot if the stock ones can take a 90 shot.  hope this helsp some.  Later.<P>Shaun||||reg||1
